  1a. Pick a quantum computer. The program asks IBM which quantum computers are free and picks the one with the shortest queue.
  It skips the weakest parts of the chip, so only the reliable parts are used: 60 qubits on ibm_marrakesh this time.

  1b. Roll the dice for 12 tumours. The quantum computer runs a small program that works like loaded dice,
  weighted by real medical statistics, to decide each tumour's size, type, edge shape and which part of the lung it sits in.
  At the same time it runs a fairness test (CHSH). That test scored 2.73, and anything above 2 counts as a pass, so the roll was kept.

  1c. Turn each roll into a personal seed number. Each tumour's dice result is mixed into a long unique code, like a fingerprint.
  Every later random choice for that tumour starts from this code, so the same run can always be replayed exactly.

  1d. Build a second, bigger quantum program. This one does three experiments side by side on separate parts of the chip:
  a chain of 28 linked qubits, a small "game of life" grid of 23 qubits, and a tiny memory machine. The game-of-life grid is the important
  one, because each tumour gets its own starting pattern on it.

  1e. Safety and cost check. The program checks that the three experiments never touch each other,
  then estimates how much quantum computer time the job will cost. It cuts the number of repeats until the cost fits under the budget
   (about 27 seconds of machine time).

  1f. Send the job. The job goes to the quantum computer with some noise-reducing settings switched on.
  The job number is saved first, so if anything crashes you can collect the results later without paying again.

  1g. Grade the three experiments. The 28-qubit chain failed its test: the qubits did not stay linked well enough.
  The game-of-life grid passed, but only barely and on one of four checks. The memory machine lost to a simple normal-computer model.

  1h. Make the tumour seeds. For each tumour, one result from the game-of-life grid is picked and turned into a tiny 3-D blob.
  That blob is the tumour's starting shape, its "seed".

  2. Grow the tumours. An ordinary computer, not the quantum one, grows each seed step by step using published rules for how lung tumours
   grow. It then paints the result into a real lung CT scan from a Siemens scanner, with that scan's own blur and grain.
   Three calcified tumours skip the seed and use a simpler built-in shape.

  3. Build the web page. Everything goes into one web page: the tumour pictures, the growth over time, and the quantum test scores.
   That is the page used for the live executive demo.
  In one sentence: the quantum computer chose what each tumour is and its starting shape, and an ordinary computer grew
  it and drew it into a real scan. The quantum part makes every case traceable and replayable; it does not make the images more realistic.